# Database Schema Documentation

This document describes the exact database schema implemented in the application models (`models.py`) and SQLite database (`app.db`).

---

## 1. Tables & Fields

### `users`
Stores user account details, credentials, and profile information.

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `user_id` | Integer | Primary Key, Auto-increment | Unique identifier for each user |
| `first_name` | String(20) | NOT NULL | User's first name |
| `last_name` | String(20) | NOT NULL | User's last name |
| `username` | String(50) | NOT NULL, UNIQUE | Unique account handle |
| `email` | String(100) | NOT NULL, UNIQUE | User email address |
| `password` | String(50) | NOT NULL | Account password |
| `gender` | String(10) | NOT NULL | User gender |
| `profile_pic` | String(200) | NULLABLE | URL path to profile picture |
| `bio` | String(400) | NOT NULL | Short author biography |
| `role` | String(10) | NOT NULL | Role designation (e.g., admin, user) |
| `created_at` | DateTime | Default: `utcnow` | Timestamp of account creation |
| `updated_at` | DateTime | Default: `utcnow` | Timestamp of last profile update |

---

### `categories`
Taxonomic structure used to organize articles.

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `category_id` | Integer | Primary Key, Auto-increment | Unique identifier for each category |
| `category_name` | String(100) | NOT NULL, UNIQUE | Name of the category |
| `icon` | String(20) | NOT NULL | Icon identifier or string code |
| `description` | String(500) | NOT NULL | Detailed category description |
| `created_at` | DateTime | Default: `utcnow` | Creation timestamp |

---

### `articles`
Stores content created and published by users.

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `article_id` | Integer | Primary Key, Auto-increment | Unique identifier for each article |
| `title` | String(100) | NOT NULL, UNIQUE | Unique article headline |
| `content` | String(2000) | NOT NULL | Main body text |
| `cover_image` | String(100) | NULLABLE | URL or path to cover image |
| `view_count` | Integer | NULLABLE | Total page views |
| `likes_count` | Integer | NULLABLE | Total likes received |
| `author_id` | Integer | Foreign Key (`users.user_id`), NOT NULL | References article author |
| `category_id` | Integer | Foreign Key (`categories.category_id`), NOT NULL | References category |
| `published_at` | DateTime | Default: `now` | Publication date and time |
| `created_at` | DateTime | Default: `utcnow` | Creation timestamp |
| `updated_at` | DateTime | Default: `utcnow` | Timestamp of last edit |

---

### `reactions`
Tracks user interaction and comments on articles.

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `reaction_id` | Integer | Primary Key, Auto-increment | Unique reaction identifier |
| `body` | String(1000) | NULLABLE | Text content or comment |
| `reaction_type` | String(100) | NULLABLE | Type of reaction (e.g., like, comment) |
| `user_id` | Integer | Foreign Key (`users.user_id`), NOT NULL | User who performed reaction |
| `article_id` | Integer | NULLABLE | Target article ID |
| `created_at` | DateTime | Default: `utcnow` | Timestamp of reaction |

---

### `follow`
Junction table managing category subscriptions by users.

| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `user_id` | Integer | Composite Primary Key, Foreign Key (`users.user_id`) | Subscribed user |
| `category_id` | Integer | Composite Primary Key, Foreign Key (`categories.category_id`) | Subscribed category |

---

## 2. Relationships Summary

* **Users -> Articles**: One-to-Many (`users.user_id` $\rightarrow$ `articles.author_id`)[cite: 11, 13].
* **Categories -> Articles**: One-to-Many (`categories.category_id` $\rightarrow$ `articles.category_id`)[cite: 11, 13].
* **Users -> Reactions**: One-to-Many (`users.user_id` $\rightarrow$ `reactions.user_id`)[cite: 11, 13].
* **Users <-> Categories (`follow`)**: Many-to-Many relationship allowing users to follow multiple categories[cite: 11, 13].